WEBVTT

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.000
What is my collection actually worth?

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.000
With Pokémon cards, that's
a surprisingly hard question.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.200
Cardelia answers it.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.020
Every card is recorded
just as it sits in the binder:

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.900
with language, condition and variant.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.600
Plus fresh prices from Cardmarket,
updated every day.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.460
So you can see at a glance how
your collection's value is changing.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.530
The catalogue holds around 80,000 cards

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.500
from more than 500 sets in four languages.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.920
The real work begins at night.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.100
That's when Cardelia fetches
fresh prices for the catalogue.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.900
If one source finds no price,
the next one steps in.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.940
Suspicious outliers are filtered out.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.380
By morning, every collection
knows its new value.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.940
Let's follow a single price
through the night.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.480
At 3 a.m. in Frankfurt,
a scheduler gives the signal.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.270
Celery takes the job
from the queue in Redis

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.280
and fetches the price.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.650
It lands in a Postgres database,

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.900
alongside more than eleven million others.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.600
In the morning, the browser asks,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.330
a Django API answers

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.320
and React extends the curve.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.810
That's how a number in the night

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.380
becomes a point on the curve in the morning.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.410
And when someone wants to know
what their collection is worth,

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.480
Cardelia already has the answer.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.060
The demo is open,
no sign-up needed.
